From 8cf95252e85d6d9a711aa8919f9c0871e73414cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 16:42:07 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: pack pixels directly into the window surface's own
 format

Per user direction (a real structural change was requested after the
render-loop-tail diagnostic confirmed the frame-time budget is now
essentially fully mapped, with no more "safe hoist" fix left): flip
(blowup + blit + present) is the single largest per-frame cost (~56ms,
edging out the render loop's ~46ms), and its largest component is
SDL_BlitSurface's format-conversion pass (~25-37ms even after patch
0013's rect-limiting).

The renderSurface + blit design exists because of a real, confirmed
real-hardware bug: patch 0003 found raw Uint32 writes (assuming 32bpp
XRGB) corrupted an actual Mach64 card, which only offers banked 24bpp,
no 32bpp mode at all (this repo's own video-corruption investigation,
2026-08-26). But the actual problem there was writing an UNVERIFIED
format, not writing the real format -- the original pre-DOS-port code
never checked what format the window surface actually negotiated
before writing into it.

This fixes that properly instead of routing around it a second time:
`blowupOntoScreenNative()` (blowUp.cpp) packs each source pixel into
the window surface's OWN native format via `SDL_MapSurfaceRGB()` (which
reads the surface's real `SDL_PixelFormatDetails` -- Rmask/Gmask/Bmask/
shifts, whatever they actually are), and writes those packed bytes
directly into the window surface's pixel buffer. This eliminates the
separate blit-conversion pass entirely -- no renderSurface intermediate,
no format mismatch, because the write is now format-aware rather than
format-assuming. Verified the byte-order convention for 3-bytes-per-
pixel writes (the case that actually matters for the real Mach64
target) against SDL's own `SDL_FillSurfaceRect3` in `SDL_fillrect.c`:
little-endian, low-byte-first -- exactly what a `memcpy` of an SDL
`Uint32`'s low N bytes already produces on this x86 target, so no
manual byte-shuffling was needed.

Only takes this fast path for 2/3/4-bytes-per-pixel destination
formats (checked once, at first flip, via `SDL_GetPixelFormatDetails`)
-- an indexed/paletted format would need a genuinely different, quality-
changing nearest-palette-color approach that's out of scope here, so
that case (and any other unexpected format) falls back to the exact
prior renderSurface + blit path unchanged, still real-hardware-verified
for that scenario. `flipScreen()`'s present step (`SDL_UpdateWindow-
Surface`/`Rects`, unaffected by this patch -- its job is flushing
window->surface->pixels to VRAM regardless of how those pixels got
populated) is factored into a shared `presentScreen()` so both the fast
path and the fallback path present identically.

FLIPDBG.LOG's log lines change shape: the old combined
`[flip-cost] blit_avg_ms=... present_avg_ms=...` line is now
`[blit-cost] blit_avg_ms=...` (fallback path only) and
`[present-cost] present_avg_ms=...` (both paths) as separate lines, plus
a new `[native-cost] native_avg_ms=...` line (fast path only, replacing
what used to be separately-timed blowup-cost + blit-cost, since this
path fuses those two steps into one).

Verified under DOSBox-X (which negotiates a real 32bpp mode, so this
DOES exercise the new fast path for real, just not the specific 3-byte-
write case): colors render correctly (no channel-swap corruption),
confirmed across two in-game blowup-factor transitions (pressed B
twice, screenshotted each) -- no corruption, no leftover pixels, same
clean letterbox behavior as every prior patch in this series.
FLIPDBG.LOG confirms the fast path is active (`[native-cost]` lines
present) at ~11-25ms/frame, comparable to the old blowup+blit combined
cost on this host. Clean boot/play/ESCAPE-quit, no hang. Real-hardware
confirmation on the 486DX2-50 -- fps number, FLIPDBG.LOG (does the
native-cost path activate for the real 24bpp Mach64 format, and does it
actually beat the old blit cost there), and explicit visual-corruption
scrutiny given this is the riskiest change in the whole investigation
(direct writes into the actual VRAM-bound surface, in a format-
sensitive way, on hardware this port can't locally emulate for this
specific bpp) -- is the essential next step before trusting this
result.
